---
title: Snippets
summary: Snippets cross an island boundary as regions. Static content freezes to server HTML. A snippet that must wake hydrates on the far side. region.snippet() is the manual form.
related:
  - regions/client-islands
  - regions/timing-nesting
---

A snippet is a function. Thus it cannot cross an island boundary directly: the props of an island are serialized, and a function does not serialize. ogygia does this for you: **each snippet given to an island crosses as a region**. That is the snippet shape of the same primitive behind `<Component/>` regions. You almost never call a function for this. Children or a `{#snippet}` passed into an island operate correctly, also when a plain wrapper forwards them.

::: tip When to use
You do not select this feature. It is what makes these operate at all: `<Tabs>{@render children()}</Tabs>`, a forwarded `{#snippet header()}`, and a wrapper component that passes content into an island. Use `region.snippet()` only when you make a snippet by hand from raw HTML.
:::

## The two shapes of a crossing

A snippet crosses in one of two ways. ogygia selects the way, based on the need to wake:

- **Static**: plain content (prose, a code block, markup with no interactivity) renders **one time on the server** and freezes to HTML. The client adopts that HTML in place. Nothing runs again, and nothing ships. This is the common case for children.
- **Live**: a named `{#snippet}` whose body must *wake* (it renders an island, or it is interactive) compiles to its own entry. It **wakes on the far side**. It renders inline on the server and hydrates on the client. Thus it is interactive where it lands, also after a boundary forwards it. Parameters are supported: the arguments of each call ride across.

```svelte
<script>
  import Tabs from './Tabs.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
</script>

<Tabs>
  <p>Frozen prose — crosses static, ships no JS.</p>

  {#snippet toolbar()}
    <LiveButton />   <!-- crosses live: interactive inside the island -->
  {/snippet}
</Tabs>
```

Nested islands inside crossed content continue to operate. They render as full regions and wake on their own, although the content around them is frozen.

## Forwarding through a wrapper

This matters because of composition. A plain (non-island) component can forward its children into an island. The crossing occurs at the boundary. A wrapper around an island is only Svelte. There is no special API:

```svelte title=CardShell.svelte
<!-- a plain wrapper -->
<script>
  import Inner from './Inner.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
  let { children } = $props();
</script>

<Inner>{@render children()}</Inner>
```

## Building one by hand: `region.snippet()`

When you need a snippet made from raw HTML, use `region.snippet()`. It is the sibling of `region()` for components. It mirrors `createRawSnippet` from Svelte. But the result is a *region* snippet: you can render it in the same graph with `{@render}`, and it can cross a boundary.

Lift an applicable snippet without parameters, to freeze it as static content:

```svelte
{#snippet badge()}<span class="badge">stable</span>{/snippet}

<script>
  import { region } from 'ogygia';
  const frozen = region.snippet(badge);
</script>
```

Or make one from raw HTML, with the same `{ render, setup?, captures? }` contract as `createRawSnippet`:

```js
import { region } from 'ogygia';

const stamp = region.snippet({
  render: (label) => `<span class="stamp">${label}</span>`,
  setup: (el) => {
    // client-only wiring; dropped when the snippet is reduced across a wire
  },
  captures: ['shipped']   // serializable inputs, same law as island props
});
```

`render` must return one root element. That is the `createRawSnippet` rule. `captures` must be serializable, the same as island props. `setup` runs on the client after mount. When the snippet crosses a boundary, `setup` is dropped. A frozen snippet has nothing to start.
